analysis:
The PR adds `DARKBLOOM_TTFT_GDN_BLOCKED` to the LaunchAgent inference passthrough allow-list, a string literal at LaunchAgent.swift head line 295, with a comment at lines 276-279. It also documents the variable in docs/reference/configuration.md at head line 414 and bumps the doc date.

Security relevance: the variable only selects a Gated DeltaNet prefill kernel. The choices are stock, a probed four-row kernel kept only if it is bit-exact, or a forced packed/rows kernel. Forcing a kernel skips the parity check, so it could change outputs. That is operator-controlled tampering with inference results, which already exists under T-007/T-012/TB-003: an operator controls the process environment and can already run in the foreground with any environment. Forwarding the variable to the launchd job therefore adds no capability an operator (ADV-001) lacks.

The passthrough does not affect:
- attestation or code identity (TB-009, TB-011);
- encryption (T-008), the X25519 key (A-003) or tokens (A-008);
- the prefix-cache gates or TTL (T-041; DARKBLOOM_PREFIX_CACHE* semantics are unchanged);
- the EnvironmentScrubber list (DYLD_* and similar are still removed).

Consumers (ADV-002) cannot set environment variables. Despite the TTFT name, the timing-oracle channel in T-041 is unchanged: kernel choice is process-wide, not prefix-dependent.

Residual question: whether forwarded LaunchAgent keys are value-validated or persisted to the plist. This is pre-existing behavior and not visible here. GatedDelta.swift itself is unchanged in this batch and was not reviewed. No regression identified.
